			<div class="content" id="post-content-3757312">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Transportation of SCP-3944 should be carried out by no more than three (3) individuals, though typically two (2) will be adequate.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't understand why people do this. Is this 'number (#)' thing some aspect of clinical tone that I don't know about? Because it really just seems like people do this because a lot of early, classic anomalies do it. I don't get it, nor do I really like it, especially since it's not done consistently. Some numbers have their numeral amended onto it and some don't. It's just&#8230; why? It breaks up the flow of the article in a weird way for me.</p>
<p>But that's kind of a minor gripe. As for the anomaly itself&#8230; I kinda like it. The pace of the article did a good job of holding my attention all the way to the end. The anomaly and the way it was presented was weird, in a good way. It was original. <em>I</em> haven't seen anything quite like it, at least. Once I had a clear understanding of what the anomaly actually did, I wasn't really sure how to feel about it, but the progressively escalating tests really held it together for me and kept me invested until the end.</p>
<p>I like it enough to upvote. The only gripes I really have with this are the 'number (#)' thing, and <em>one</em> SCP-XXXX that was left behind at the end of Test Log 01. Other than that, good job, me likey.</p>

							</div>

								
					
