					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lt-flops" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1735419); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1735419&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606273916" alt="Lt Flops" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1735419)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lt-flops" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1735419); return false;" >Lt Flops</a></span> <span class="odate time_1523399964 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Apr 2018 22:39</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3765302">
									

<p>This is interesting for a first scip, but quite honestly I wasn't feeling it. Altogether I think there could have been further adjustments. The biggest issue I have with this scip is that it hasn't been tightened to fit a clinical tone. That's vague so I'll explain what I mean by that.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">crit,&nbsp;click&nbsp;me!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Description: SCP-3944 is an outdoor patio table from ███████. The object is a iron round table, matte black in color, approximately 80cm in height with a radius of 61cm, with a surface being made of 5mm tempered glass.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Right away, this description has some redundant information. Clinical tone is concerned with displaying information in a way that's easy to understand while still remaining practical. This first paragraph could easily be shortened to a single sentence to achieve that end:</p>
<p>&quot;SCP-3944 is a black iron patio table measuring 80&#160;cm in height and 122&#160;cm in width, with a 5&#160;mm thick tempered glass surface.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-3944 causes a &quot;loop&quot; of interrupted thought processes. It is expressed as an instance of SCP-3944-1 stopping mid conversation to ask the instance to their immediate left if they can recall the topic of conversation, having a momentary lapse in train of thought mid-sentence. It is at this point a repetitive looping effect takes place. In a steady and consistent, sequential clockwise order, instances of SCP-3944-1 will begin repeating a series of questions and replies to each other. This will not stop until either an instance is removed from SCP-3944's radius, causing the number of subjects to fall below the minimum requirement for activation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Honestly, I had to reread this paragraph a couple times to understand what it was trying to describe. So, when people sit at the table, they become influenced by the mind-affecting properties, becoming SCP-3944-1. After this, their memory and attention span become shortened, and they're unable to leave the table. I think you could manage to cut out a bit of extraneous explanation here to make it more easily understood.</p>
<p>I will say that I think this scip suffers from a lack of critiques. You did get one critique, which is good, but you should look into getting even more eyes on a work before posting it. Also, try to prioritize getting crits from members that have written successful articles, because they may have a better idea for the flow of things.</p>
<p>Other than the issues I had with the description, I found the dialogue in the incident log to be a bit stilted, and it didn't read like real people talking. I suggest reading the dialogue out loud to gauge how you could change it to sound less awkward and more real.</p>
<p>I also found all but the final testing log to be unnecessary. I'm more interested in the greater meaning behind these mind-affecting properties, not how the Foundation came to understand them. You did attempt to tell a greater story in the final testing log, but it didn't feel like it led anywhere, nor did it evoke an emotional reaction from me.</p>
<p>Final thoughts: this is a play on a &quot;round table&quot; discussion, isn't it?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>It's not a bad concept, mind you, I just think it should have gone through few more crits to shape it into something great before posting. So unfortunately that's a downvote from me. Good luck.</p>

